WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.287
Performance tuning starts by separating prompt processing speed from token generation speed.

00:00:06.287 --> 00:00:10.319
Prompt processing is the work of reading the input context.

00:00:10.319 --> 00:00:14.419
Token generation is the step-by-step creation of the answer.

00:00:14.419 --> 00:00:19.271
A system may feel fast with short prompts but slow with long documents.

00:00:19.271 --> 00:00:27.539
Measuring both phases helps identify whether the issue is context size, model size, runtime settings, or hardware limits.

00:00:27.539 --> 00:00:30.956
Context window size has a direct operational cost.

00:00:30.956 --> 00:00:38.746
Larger context can help document analysis and long conversations, but it increases memory use and processing time.

00:00:38.746 --> 00:00:41.685
Larger models are not always better either.

00:00:41.685 --> 00:00:47.698
A model that barely fits may swap memory, run slowly, or become unstable under real use.

00:00:47.698 --> 00:00:54.122
A slightly smaller model with a sensible context setting may produce a better user experience.

00:00:54.122 --> 00:01:05.671
GPU acceleration, batch concepts, thread settings, and runtime parameters can influence performance, but changing too many settings at once makes troubleshooting harder.

00:01:05.671 --> 00:01:11.889
Establish a baseline with a known model, known prompt, context length, and runtime version.

00:01:11.889 --> 00:01:14.281
Then change one variable at a time.

00:01:14.281 --> 00:01:21.456
Record the effect on load time, prompt processing, generation speed, memory pressure, and output quality.

00:01:21.456 --> 00:01:25.761
Thermal throttling and memory pressure are practical realities.

00:01:25.761 --> 00:01:31.228
A laptop under sustained load may reduce performance to manage heat and battery.

00:01:31.228 --> 00:01:35.602
A system under memory pressure may slow down or become unstable.

00:01:35.602 --> 00:01:40.932
Monitor disk, memory, temperature, and service behavior during real workloads.

00:01:40.932 --> 00:01:49.064
The best tuning process is measured, repeatable, and tied to the task rather than chasing the largest possible numbers.
